{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "בהר סיני", :translit "b'har sinai", :gloss "on Mount Sinai"} {:he "מקלל", :translit "m'kalel", :gloss "blasphemer"} {:he "לא תשא", :translit "lo tisa", :gloss "you shall not take [the Name in vain]"} {:he "בן ארבע אותיות", :translit "ben arba otot", :gloss "a matter of four letters"} {:he "שמיטה", :translit "sh'mittah", :gloss "the Sabbatical year"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition (semikhut) of the blasphemer pericope to 'on Mount Sinai'"} {:kind :other, :note "Second juxtaposition: Shemittah section linked to Sinai"}], :frame {:text "Leviticus 24:10–23 recounts the incident of the blasphemer (מקלל), followed immediately at Leviticus 25:1 by the words 'on Mount Sinai' introducing the laws of Shemittah. The Ba'al HaTurim reads the sequential placement as intentional juxtaposition (semikhut). The Sinaitic revelation is described as causing cosmic trembling at Exodus 19:16–18 and parallels such as Psalms 68:8–9 and Judges 5:5. The commandment 'you shall not take the Name in vain' (לא תשא) is given at Sinai in Exodus 20:7. The reference to Kiddushin addresses the teaching of the Divine Name: Kiddushin 71a states that the Name of four letters was given over to students once a week, and that 'a matter of four letters' sages hand over to their students once a week.", :sources ["Leviticus 24:10–23" "Leviticus 25:1" "Exodus 19:16–18" "Exodus 20:7" "Psalms 68:8–9" "Judges 5:5" "Kiddushin 71a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition (semikhut) of the blasphemer pericope to 'on Mount Sinai'", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Second juxtaposition: Shemittah section linked to Sinai", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:25:26.856980525Z", :model "glm-5.2"}